#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:25
msgid "Usage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:27
msgid "**Simple usage**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:29
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"import { uniformInt } from 'pure-rand/distribution/uniformInt';\n"
"import { xoroshiro128plus } from 'pure-rand/generator/xoroshiro128plus';\n"
"\n"
"const seed = 42;\n"
"const rng = xoroshiro128plus(seed);\n"
"const firstDiceValue = uniformInt(rng, 1, 6); // value in {1..6}, here: 2"
"\n"
"const secondDiceValue = uniformInt(rng, 1, 6); // value in {1..6}, here: "
"4\n"
"const thirdDiceValue = uniformInt(rng, 1, 6); // value in {1..6}, here: 6"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:40
msgid "**Pure usage**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:42
msgid ""
"Pure means that the instance `rng` will never be altered in-place. It can"
" be called again and again and it will always return the same value. But "
"it will also return the next `rng`. Here is an example showing how the "
"code above can be translated into its pure version:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:44
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"import { uniformIntDistribution } from 'pure-"
"rand/distribution/UniformIntDistribution';\n"
"import { xoroshiro128plus } from 'pure-rand/generator/xoroshiro128plus';\n"
"import { purify } from 'pure-rand/utils/purify';\n"
"\n"
"const uniformIntDistributionPure = purify(uniformIntDistribution);\n"
"\n"
"const seed = 42;\n"
"const rng1 = xoroshiro128plus(seed);\n"
"const [firstDiceValue, rng2] = uniformIntDistributionPure(rng1, 1, 6); //"
" value in {1..6}, here: 2\n"
"const [secondDiceValue, rng3] = uniformIntDistributionPure(rng2, 1, 6); "
"// value in {1..6}, here: 4\n"
"const [thirdDiceValue, rng4] = uniformIntDistributionPure(rng3, 1, 6); //"
" value in {1..6}, here: 6\n"
"\n"
"// You can call: uniformIntDistributionPure(rng1, 1, 6);\n"
"// over and over it will always give you back the same value along with a"
" new rng (always producing the same values too).\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:61
msgid "**Independent simulations**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:63
msgid ""
"In order to produce independent simulations it can be tempting to "
"instanciate several PRNG based on totally different seeds. While it would"
" produce distinct set of values, the best way to ensure fully unrelated "
"sequences is rather to use jumps. Jump just consists into moving far away"
" from the current position in the generator (eg.: jumping in Xoroshiro "
"128+ will move you 2<sup>64</sup> generations away from the current one "
"on a generator having a sequence of 2<sup>128</sup> elements)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:65
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"import { uniformInt } from 'pure-rand/distribution/uniformInt';\n"
"import { xoroshiro128plus } from 'pure-rand/generator/xoroshiro128plus';\n"
"import { purify } from 'pure-rand/utils/purify';\n"
"\n"
"const pureJump = purify((rng) => rng.jump());\n"
"const createAnotherSimulation = (rng) => pureJump(rng)[1];\n"
"\n"
"const seed = 42;\n"
"const rngSimulation1 = xoroshiro128plus(seed);\n"
"const rngSimulation2 = createAnotherSimulation(rngSimulation1); // not "
"in-place, creates a new instance\n"
"const rngSimulation3 = createAnotherSimulation(rngSimulation2); // not "
"in-place, creates a new instance\n"
"\n"
"const diceSim1Value = uniformInt(rngSimulation1, 1, 6); // value in "
"{1..6}, here: 2\n"
"const diceSim2Value = uniformInt(rngSimulation2, 1, 6); // value in "
"{1..6}, here: 5\n"
"const diceSim3Value = uniformInt(rngSimulation3, 1, 6); // value in "
"{1..6}, here: 6\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:83
msgid "**Non-uniform usage**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:85
msgid ""
"While not recommended as non-uniform distribution implies that one or "
"several values from the range will be more likely than others, it might "
"be tempting for people wanting to maximize the throughput."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:87
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"import { xoroshiro128plus } from 'pure-rand/generator/xoroshiro128plus';\n"
"\n"
"const seed = 42;\n"
"const rng = xoroshiro128plus(seed);\n"
"const rand = (min, max) => {\n"
"  const out = (rng.next() >>> 0) / 0x100000000;\n"
"  return min + Math.floor(out * (max - min + 1));\n"
"};\n"
"const firstDiceValue = rand(1, 6); // value in {1..6}, here: 6\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:99
msgid "**Select your seed**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:101
msgid ""
"While not perfect, here is a rather simple way to generate a seed for "
"your PNRG."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:103
msgid "const seed = Date.now() ^ (Math.random() * 0x100000000);\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:107
msgid "Documentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:109
msgid "Pseudorandom number generators"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:111
msgid ""
"In computer science most random number generators<sup>(1)</sup> are "
"[pseudorandom number "
"generators](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pseudorandom_number_generator) "
"(abbreviated: PRNG). In other words, they are fully deterministic and "
"given the original seed one can rebuild the whole sequence."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:113
msgid ""
"Each PRNG algorithm has to deal with tradeoffs in terms of randomness "
"quality, speed, length of the sequence<sup>(2)</sup>... In other words, "
"it's important to compare relative speed of libraries with that in mind. "
"Indeed, a Mersenne Twister PRNG will not have the same strenghts and "
"weaknesses as a Xoroshiro PRNG, so depending on what you need exactly you"
" might prefer one PRNG over another even if it will be slower."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:115
msgid "4 PRNGs come with pure-rand:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:117
msgid ""
"`congruential32`: Linear Congruential generator — "
"\\[[more](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Linear_congruential_generator)\\]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:118
msgid ""
"`mersenne`: Mersenne Twister generator — "
"\\[[more](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mersenne_Twister)\\]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:119
msgid ""
"`xorshift128plus`: Xorshift 128+ generator — "
"\\[[more](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xorshift)\\]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:120
msgid ""
"`xoroshiro128plus`: Xoroshiro 128+ generator — "
"\\[[more](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xorshift)\\]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:122
msgid ""
"Our recommendation is `xoroshiro128plus`. But if you want to use another "
"one, you can replace it by any other PRNG provided by pure-rand in the "
"examples above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:124
msgid ""
"Each of these generators come with its own import: `pure-"
"rand/generator/<name>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:126
msgid "Distributions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:128
msgid ""
"Once you are able to generate random values, next step is to scale them "
"into the range you want. Indeed, you probably don't want a floating point"
" value between 0 (included) and 1 (excluded) but rather an integer value "
"between 1 and 6 if you emulate a dice or any other range based on your "
"needs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:130
msgid ""
"At this point, simple way would be to do `min + floor(random() * (max - "
"min + 1))` but actually it will not generate the values with equal "
"probabilities even if you use the best PRNG in the world to back "
"`random()`. In order to have equal probabilities you need to rely on "
"uniform distributions<sup>(3)</sup> which comes built-in in some PNRG "
"libraries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:132
msgid ""
"pure-rand provides 4 built-in functions for uniform distributions of "
"values:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:134
msgid "`uniformInt(rng, min, max)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:135
msgid "`uniformBigInt(rng, min, max)` - with `min` and `max` being `bigint`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:136
msgid ""
"`uniformFloat32(rng)` - to generate value between 0 (included) and 1 "
"(excluded)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:137
msgid ""
"`uniformFloat64(rng)` - to generate value between 0 (included) and 1 "
"(excluded)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:139
msgid ""
"Each of these distributions come with its own import: `pure-"
"rand/distribution/<name>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:141
msgid "Extra helpers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:143
msgid ""
"Some helpers are also provided in order to ease the use of "
"`RandomGenerator` instances:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:145
msgid ""
"`generateN(rng: RandomGenerator, num: number): number[]`: generates `num`"
" random values using `rng` and return them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:146
msgid ""
"`skipN(rng: RandomGenerator, num: number): void`: skips `num` random "
"values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:148
msgid ""
"And one last helper responsible to change any function accepting an "
"instance of `RandomGenerator` as a first argument into a pure version of "
"it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:150
msgid ""
"`purify<TArgs extends unknown[], TReturn>(action: (rng: RandomGenerator, "
"...args: TArgs) => TReturn): (rng: RandomGenerator, ...args: TArgs) => "
"[TReturn, RandomGenerator]`: purifies the action"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:152
msgid "Each of these helpers come with its own import: `pure-rand/utils/<name>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:154
msgid "Comparison"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:156
msgid ""
"pure-rand offers competitive performance compared to other PRNG "
"libraries, with built-in support for uniform distributions — ensuring "
"unbiased results out of the box."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:160
msgid ""
"For detailed benchmark results and methodology, see the [full "
"comparison](./COMPARISON.md)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:164
msgid ""
"(1) — Not all as there are also [hardware-based random number "
"generator](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hardware_random_number_generator)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:166
msgid "(2) — How long it takes to reapeat itself?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/node_modules/pure-rand/README.md:168
msgid ""
"(3) — While most users don't really think of it, uniform distribution is "
"key! Without it entries might be biased towards some values and make some"
" others less probable. The naive `rand() % numValues` is a good example "
"of biased version as if `rand()` is uniform in `0, 1, 2` and `numValues` "
"is `2`, the probabilities are: `P(0) = 67%`, `P(1) = 33%` causing `1` to "
"be less probable than `0`"
msgstr ""

